Component importance (in order):
  1. Semantic priors (RSN) provide the largest benefit
  2. Task coordination is crucial for avoiding redundant search
  3. Belief fusion enables faster convergence
  4. Intention sharing prevents immediate conflicts
  5. DSG sharing provides modest improvement

6.9 ENVIRONMENT COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS
-----------------------------------

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ TABLE 6: Performance Across Environment Sizes (3 agents)                    │
├─────────────────┬───────────┬──────────┬──────────┬─────────┬───────────────┤
│ Environment     │ Rooms     │ SR       │ SPL      │ Steps   │ Speedup       │
├─────────────────┼───────────┼──────────┼──────────┼─────────┼───────────────┤
│ Small Office    │ 12        │ 98%      │ 0.82     │ 28      │ 2.71×         │
│ Medium Building │ 24        │ 97%      │ 0.78     │ 49      │ 2.59×         │
│ Large Facility  │ 48        │ 94%      │ 0.73     │ 87      │ 2.48×         │
└─────────────────┴───────────┴──────────┴──────────┴─────────┴───────────────┘

SEEK-Multi maintains strong performance across environment sizes, with speedup 
remaining above 2.4× even for 48-room facilities.


================================================================================
                              7. DISCUSSION
================================================================================

7.1 SCALABILITY CONSIDERATIONS
------------------------------

Communication Overhead:
Message volume grows with O(N²) for full connectivity. For teams larger than 
6-8 agents, potential solutions include:
  • Hierarchical communication with local leaders
  • Sparse communication graphs limited to k-nearest neighbors
  • Attention-based prioritization using learned mechanisms

Coordination Complexity:
Task allocation becomes combinatorially harder with more agents. Alternative 
approaches for very large teams include:
  • Spatial decomposition: Partition environment and assign teams to regions
  • Hierarchical task allocation: Two-level with region then room assignment
  • Learning-based coordination: Multi-agent reinforcement learning

Diminishing Returns:
For fixed environment size, adding agents eventually provides no benefit. In our 
24-room medium environment, diminishing returns appear beyond 4-5 agents. Larger 
environments can benefit from larger teams.

7.2 HETEROGENEOUS TEAMS
-----------------------

Agent capabilities are modeled through:
  • Sensor parameters: Detection range, accuracy, field of view
  • Mobility parameters: Speed, traversability constraints
  • Communication parameters: Range, bandwidth

Tasks are preferentially assigned to capable agents through modified bidding:

                    Priority(v) · Capabilityᵢ(v)
    Bidᵢ(v) = ──────────────────────────────────── · LoadFactorᵢ
                          Costᵢ(v)

Mixed Teams:
A 2-ground + 1-drone team achieved 15% better SPL than a 3-ground team in large 
facility environment, demonstrating the value of heterogeneity.

7.3 DEPLOYMENT CONSIDERATIONS
-----------------------------

Initialization:
  • Pre-loading from a common map server
  • Incremental sharing during synchronization phase
  • Graceful handling of partial initialization

Real-Time Performance:
  • Belief updates: O(M) per observation
  • Task allocation: O(M log M) per round
  • Path planning: O(M²) using Dijkstra's algorithm
  • All components run in real-time with <50ms per step

7.4 LIMITATIONS AND FUTURE WORK
-------------------------------

Current Limitations:
  • Semantic priors trained on common object-room relationships
  • Communication model assumes reliable delivery within range
  • Static environments assumed
  • Simulation-based evaluation only

Future Directions:
  • Real-world deployment on physical robot teams
  • Learned communication strategies
  • Dynamic environments with moving objects
  • Hierarchical coordination for 10+ agents
  • Human-robot teaming
  • Active learning for updating semantic priors


================================================================================
                              8. CONCLUSION
================================================================================

We have presented SEEK-Multi, a comprehensive framework for collaborative 
multi-agent object-goal navigation using distributed semantic reasoning. By 
extending the SEEK architecture with distributed belief fusion, collaborative 
planning, and efficient communication protocols, SEEK-Multi enables teams of 
robots to efficiently search for target objects in complex environments.

KEY TECHNICAL CONTRIBUTIONS:
  • A distributed belief fusion algorithm based on consensus protocols with 
    provable convergence guarantees
  • An auction-based task allocation mechanism that incorporates semantic priors 
    for improved efficiency
  • A communication protocol that balances information sharing with bandwidth 
    constraints
  • Support for both centralized and decentralized coordination modes

EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS DEMONSTRATE:
  • Near-linear speedup with up to 4 agents (3.1× speedup with 4 agents)
  • High success rates maintained across configurations (94-97%)
  • Graceful degradation under communication failures (distributed mode 
    maintains 91% of baseline SPL at 50% message loss)
  • Significant improvement over uncoordinated and non-semantic baselines 
    (37-69% faster)
  • Consistent performance across environment sizes and configurations

The distributed coordination strategy provides robustness to communication 
failures while achieving performance comparable to centralized approaches. 
Ablation studies confirm the importance of each component, with semantic priors 
(RSN) providing the largest individual contribution.

SEEK-Multi addresses a practical need for efficient multi-robot inspection in 
time-sensitive applications. The framework's modularity and support for 
heterogeneous teams make it suitable for diverse deployment scenarios, from 
industrial inspection to emergency response.

We believe SEEK-Multi represents a significant step toward practical multi-robot 
semantic inspection systems that can operate efficiently in complex, real-world 
environments.


